result.summary:
Refused. The rung is well measured. The recorded failure predates the edit, and the provenance block holds. The count (75) and the distance (626) match the landed gate site for site, the ladder is unchanged, and my own seeded suite-shaped run of the rung's 19 .test files passes 44 of 44.

The refusal: the rung does not rank Σ′ whole. A generic declaration reached only by coercion is never compared with a plain declaration reached only by coercion, because the first attempt already holds the plain ones and is kept. So SkSigmaMore runs the less specific g(W2, Any), and SkSigmaTie compiles where its all-plain twin is refused. Both contradict decision 1 and the rung's own FACTS line.

Required corrections for the repair round:
- the numeral tie's magnitude reading looks only among the tied maximal candidates, so pickn(3000000000) is refused beside a pickn(ZZ64);
- InferCoercionShapes.fss:50 pins row 442's prelude answer ZZ as expected;
- the f(x) fallback reports a call the rule accepts as not applicable (SkCtxMsg);
- an untyped lambda argument and the 64-combination cap leave row 401's shape refused, and each is owed a test;
- the record overstates row 391's closure and omits c3's changed message;
- the newly accepted c1 compiles and fails JVM verification (SkBottomRun).

No slowdown was measured back to back (97/99 s against 99/104 s). SKEPTIC.md and the probes are committed at 13ba3f3f0 on wip/rung-inference-checker and pushed.

result.ruling:
The skeptic is right on its refusal ground.
- Functionals.scala:640 makes applicable(context, false) the first attempt.
- A candidate without static parameters goes to checkApplicableWithoutInference in either mode (:162-169), which builds coercions (:442-449). So the first attempt holds the plain declarations applicable only with coercion.
- kept (:633-637) accepts it, so the coercion attempt (:640), the only place a generic is admitted with coercion, is not reached.
- The specification defines Σ and Σ′ over all declarations (Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex:533-553).
- Decision 1 (POSITIONS 2026-09-28, the two decisions of Fable's judgement) reads them on declared, quantified domains, "a generic declaration fits when some instance within its bound fits". The judgement's resolution makes a tie there a static error (explorations/reviews/conversion-overloading-judgement.md:111).
- Measured: SkSigmaMore.diff.txt:6 (g(W2, Any)); SkSigmaTie.diff.txt:4-6 (compiles, f(B, Any)) against SkSigmaTiePlain.diff.txt:5 (refused).
- The worker's defence fails. Decision 3 (POSITIONS 2026-09-27) orders the context and its retry, not which declarations are compared together.

The judge adds one point the skeptic did not name.
- With generics in Σ′, moreSpecificCandidate compares a coercion-attempt generic on its instance (STypesUtil.scala:1102), since only promoted candidates carry their declared arrow (Functionals.scala:626).
- Example: h[\T\](W2, T) beside h(W2, ZZ64), called h(NOf(1), 3), would run the generic at IntLiteral. IntLiteral excludes ZZ64 (CompilerBuiltin.fsi:390), so it wins by ⪯ (conversions-coercions.tex:494-499). Decision 1's declared domains take the plain declaration.
- So the Σ′ generic carries its declared arrow, with its coercions counted.

The skeptic's corrections, and the one claim it did not raise:
- numeralTie sees only top (Functionals.scala:683). SkNumeralBig is refused (diff.txt:5), where the judgement's section 4 reading ZZ64 fits pickn(ZZ64). Refined: the reading resolves by the coercion chapter's order over every candidate (:472-476).
- InferCoercionShapes.fss:50 pins row 442's prelude answer ZZ, where the specification says ZZ64 (Specification/basic-lib/basic-integers.tex:25, :36-37), and so do answer 8 and the batch record's test list. The worker's measurement stands; the assertion moves to an XXX test (the precedent is POSITIONS 2026-09-24, row 383).
- The f(x) fallback (:643-645) reports a call the fourth attempt accepts as not applicable (SkCtxMsg.diff.txt:6).
- The untyped lambda (:303) and the 64-combination cap (:356) are specification-settled faces of row 401 (conversions-coercions.tex:95-98, :417-432), so they get home-2 XXX tests.
- Row 391's specification answer is refusing the declarations (Specification/advanced/overloading.tex:196-216, :247-273), which is not built, so the row stays open.
- c3's message changes under TestsD (SolverResultOnly.base.txt:12-14 against .after.txt:9-11).
- c1 now fails JVM verification (SkBottomRun.diff.txt:4-6).
- The citations :842 and :1028 should be :838 and :1024 (checked).
- Row 455's method, prefix, infix and parenthesised faces are now accepted (SkCtxForms.diff.txt).
- Not raised by the skeptic: row 455 cannot close whole. Its own claim's argument face (takesBox64(wrapT(3)); measurement D section 1.3) and the loose juxtaposition (SkCtxForms.fss:28) still drop the context. The specification settles both (Specification/basic/expressions/var-ref.tex:35-40), so they get an XXX test.

The worker is right, and the skeptic confirms it, on:
- the recorded failure;
- the provenance;
- the count, 75, and the distance, 626, site for site;
- the ladder;
- the promotion, its lookup and the ranking of a promoted candidate on its declared domain;
- the kept-if-it-converts rule;
- the maximal-element tie check;
- the judgement's five programs.

The worker is wrong on:
- its FACTS claim that a converted tie is refused (SkSigmaTie);
- the numeral tie by magnitude;
- the ZZ assertion;
- the fallback's truthfulness;
- closing rows 391 and 455 whole;
- the omissions in the record.

instructions:
Edit only Functionals.scala and STypesUtil.scala.
- The Ranked entry becomes (AppCandidate, declared: Option[ArrowType], promoted: Boolean). In applicable (Functionals.scala:622-631):
  - the promoted case is (Left(promoted), Some(pc.arrow), true);
  - in the coercion attempt, a candidate whose pc.arrow has non-lifted static parameters is (Left(c), Some(pc.arrow), false);
  - everything else is (e, None, false).
- Update Attempt, candidatesOf, moreSpecific (:613-615, :632) and the unfixed-size check (:661-664) to match.

In moreSpecificCandidate (STypesUtil.scala:1071-1104), add promoted1 and promoted2 (default false), and let coercionK = !promotedK && argsK.exists(_.isInstanceOf[CoercionInvocation]). Then:
- (true, false) gives false and (false, true) gives true, as now;
- (false, false) with a declared arrow on either side: moreSpecificDeclared, as built;
- (true, true) with a declared arrow on either side, taking dK = declaredK.getOrElse(candidateK.arrow): true if coercions.moreSpecificDeclared(d1, d2), false if moreSpecificDeclared(d2, d1), otherwise coercions.moreSpecific(newDomain1, newDomain2);
- with no declared arrow on either side, the team's code unchanged, so the two-argument caller at CoercionOracle.scala:252 keeps today's behaviour.

numeralTie (Functionals.scala:683, :690-708): keep its gate over top, namely that the tied candidates differ only at numeral arguments and each has a reading. Then choose over every candidate of the kept attempt:
- a candidate fits when, at every numeral argument position, that numeral's numeralReading is substitutable for the candidate's parameter type there;
- for a candidate with a declared arrow, at a position whose declared type is a bare type parameter, it fits when the reading is substitutable for that parameter's bound;
- of the fits, those the readings are subtypes of at every numeral position come first; take that set's single element that no other in it is more specific than, by moreSpecific;
- if that set is empty, take the same among all fits;
- if there is no single such element, signalAmbiguity over top, as now.
Leave alone the solver, Operators.scala, CoercionOracle.scala, TraitTable.scala, and the cap and lambda refusal at Functionals.scala:303 and :356.

result.loudToQuiet:
Yes, by design. The "Ambiguous coercion" refusals become computed answers: 16 calls in the worker's matrix and 3 more of mine (EdgeNN64NN32Max, EdgeZZZZ64Max, EqualZZ64ZZ32Max). In addition, 25 matrix answers and 10 of mine change type from QQ to an integer type.

The new value is the larger or smaller argument (for MINMAX, the ordered pair), converted to the narrowest type both arguments coerce into. I checked it against basic-integers.tex:642-645 on:
- unsigned values above the signed half, where the unsigned order is kept;
- a ZZ of about 2^93;
- negative values;
- equal arguments.

Every value is the specification's, and no value changed. The loud failure reported a defect of the library's overload set, not a user error, so no diagnostic a user needs is lost. The quiet residue is the type change from QQ to an integer type; no output of the 429 interpreter tests changed from it.
